{"prdMarkdown":"# PRD: Dashboard Internationalization (i18n)\n\n## 1. Problem\nThe internal operations dashboard currently supports only a single hardcoded language (English). Ops teams and dashboard users who prefer or require Spanish cannot switch language contexts, creating friction and reducing operational efficiency across multi-lingual teams.\n\n## 2. Goals\n- Introduce internationalization support to the dashboard, starting with English (`en`) and Spanish (`es`).\n- Provide a client-side mechanism to switch languages directly within the UI.\n- Persist user language preferences entirely client-side across browser sessions.\n- Maintain seamless fallback behavior so users never encounter blank UI elements due to missing translations.\n\n## 3. Non-goals\n- Backend user preference persistence (the dashboard currently has no dedicated backend service to store user profiles).\n- Languages beyond English and Spanish for this initial release.\n- Complex automated server-side locale detection or geo-IP routing.\n\n## 4. User Impact\n- **Target Audience:** Internal ops team members using the dashboard.\n- **User Experience:** Users can select their preferred language (English or Spanish) via a language switcher in `src/components/Header.jsx`. The interface will immediately update to display translated UI strings and persist this choice on subsequent visits via client-side storage.\n\n## 5. Open Product Questions\n- Does the scope of internationalization include date, time, currency, and number formatting, or is it strictly limited to static UI string translation (clarification skipped during intake)?\n- What should be the default locale behavior for first-time visitors (detecting via `navigator.language` vs. defaulting strictly to English)?\n- What is the expected fallback behavior when a translation key is missing in Spanish (defaulting to the English string vs. rendering the translation key)?","trdMarkdown":"# TRD: Dashboard Internationalization (i18n)\n\n## 1. Architecture Notes\n- **Current Tech Stack:** React 18 (`react` `^18.2.0`) with `react-router-dom` `^6.20.0` as identified in `package.json`. No existing internationalization library is installed.\n- **Library Selection & Provider:** \n  - Integrate an i18n solution (such as `react-i18next` with `i18next`, or a lightweight custom React Context provider) to manage active locale state and dictionary lookups.\n  - Wrap top-level routes in `src/App.jsx` with the i18n provider so translation hooks/utilities are globally available.\n- **State Persistence:**\n  - Implement client-side persistence using browser `localStorage` to save the selected language key (`en` or `es`).\n  - On initial load in `src/App.jsx`, initialize state by checking `localStorage`, falling back to `navigator.language` or `en`.\n- **UI Integration:**\n  - Update `src/components/Header.jsx` to render a language switcher component (e.g., dropdown or toggle button) that dispatches locale updates.\n- **Translation Assets:**\n  - Introduce structured translation dictionaries (e.g., JSON resource files) for English and Spanish locales containing keyed UI copy.\n\n## 2. Edge Cases & Dependencies\n- **Missing Translation Keys:** If a translation key is missing in the Spanish dictionary, the translation engine must gracefully fall back to the English string to prevent raw keys or blank elements from rendering.\n- **Dependencies (`package.json`):** Evaluating whether adding `i18next` / `react-i18next` is preferable to an in-house React Context wrapper to minimize bundle overhead vs. leveraging mature pluralization and formatting features.\n- **Date and Number Formatting:** If dashboard metrics or logs include formatted timestamps or numbers, string-only replacement will leave dates/numbers unlocalized unless standard `Intl.DateTimeFormat` and `Intl.NumberFormat` APIs are leveraged.\n- **API Responses (`src/api/client.js`):** Confirm whether error messages or responses from `src/api/client.js` require localization or if internationalization is strictly confined to client-side static strings.\n\n## 3. Engineering Questions\n- Which internationalization library or architecture should be adopted (e.g., `react-i18next`, `formatjs`/`react-intl`, or a lightweight custom React Context implementation)?\n- How should translation resource bundles be loaded (bundled synchronously in the main build or loaded asynchronously via dynamic imports)?\n\n## 4. Estimate\n- **Expected:** 2-3 days\n- **Worst Case:** Up to 7 days (accounting for dictionary extraction across components and formatting requirements)"}